# pca (Phoenix)

Computes the mean vector and the covariance matrix of a randomly generated
matrix (the first step of principal component analysis).

## Benchmark source

The Phoenix source code is distributed with a BSD license. The copyright is
held by Stanford University. Phoenix is provided "as is" without any
guarantees of any kind. Please contact Christos Kozyrakis
(kozyraki@stanford.edu) for further information.

Repository: https://github.com/kozyraki/phoenix/

## Build

```bash
make
```

`make` builds `pca_pim.exe`, `pca_pim_fast.exe`, `pca_baseline.exe`.

| Executable | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `pca_baseline.exe` | CPU baseline |
| `pca_pim.exe` | PUD-instrumented version; also executes every bbop on the CPU |
| `pca_pim_fast.exe` | PUD-instrumented version without CPU execution of bbops (used by `scripts/run_workloads.sh`) |

## Run

```
pca_<version>.exe -r <num_rows> -c <num_cols> [-s <max_value>]
```

`-s` sets the maximum random value used to initialize the matrix. The paper
uses:

```bash
./pca_pim_fast.exe -r 16000 -c 16000
```

## Output

The PUD versions write `bbop_statistics.csv`, `transposition.csv`, and
`bbop_resolution.csv` to the working directory. See `../README.md` for their
format.
